  <section class="msv__section">
    <div class="msv__container--narrow">
      <div class="msv__section-hd">
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
        <p>Get answers to the questions we hear most from renewal applicants.</p>
      </div>

      <details class="msv__faq-list">
        <summary>When should I apply to renew my Spanish residence permit?</summary>
        <div class="msv__faq-answer">
          <p>You can apply starting 60 days before your current permit expires. You have until 90 days after expiration to submit your application. It is critical to apply within this window. Missing the deadline can result in losing your residency status entirely.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="msv__faq-list">
        <summary>What is the cost to renew my Spanish residence permit?</summary>
        <div class="msv__faq-answer">
          <p>The official administrative fee for renewing your TIE card is €16.32, paid via the Tasa 790-052 (Official Bulletin). If you hire an immigration lawyer, expect additional professional fees of €500–€1,500 depending on your visa complexity.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="msv__faq-list">
        <summary>How long does the renewal process take?</summary>
        <div class="msv__faq-answer">
          <p>Processing typically takes 30–60 days after you submit your application and provide biometrics. However, this varies by province and immigration office workload. Plan for 45 days as a safe estimate.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="msv__faq-list">
        <summary>Do I need an appointment (cita previa) to renew?</summary>
        <div class="msv__faq-answer">
          <p>Yes. You must schedule a cita previa at your local Delegación de Extranjería. Appointments fill up quickly, especially near renewal deadlines. Book as early as possible once you enter the eligibility window (60 days before expiry).</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="msv__faq-list">
        <summary>Can I renew after my permit expires?</summary>
        <div class="msv__faq-answer">
          <p>Yes, but with caution. Spanish law allows renewal up to 90 days after expiration. However, your legal status during this post-expiration period is ambiguous. Always aim to apply before your permit expires to avoid legal complications.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="msv__faq-list">
        <summary>What happens if I miss the renewal deadline?</summary>
        <div class="msv__faq-answer">
          <p>If you miss the 60-days-before to 90-days-after window, you lose your residency status. You would then need to apply for a completely new visa from outside Spain, which is far more expensive and time-consuming. Do not miss the deadline.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="msv__faq-list">
        <summary>Do I need a digital certificate to renew?</summary>
        <div class="msv__faq-answer">
          <p>A digital certificate is not strictly required, but having one can be useful for managing online administrative tasks and downloading official documents. It is highly recommended for convenience.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="msv__faq-list">
        <summary>Can I renew online or do I need to visit the immigration office in person?</summary>
        <div class="msv__faq-answer">
          <p>You must visit the immigration office in person for your initial submission and biometric appointment (fingerprints and photo). Some paperwork can be prepared online, but you cannot complete the entire process remotely.</p>
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
  </section>

